::CW_U05_L07_GP_Q01::Portfolio context: A writer chooses a story only because it is the shortest draft. What is the best correction?{
~A. Choose it automatically because short always means portfolio-ready.#Incorrect. Length does not prove readiness.
~B. Choose the first draft found and skip revision.#Incorrect. A portfolio piece needs a reason and revision evidence.
=C. Choose a piece because it shows growth, strong craft, or clear publication potential.#Correct. Portfolio selection should be purposeful.
~D. Choose no piece because portfolios cannot include creative work.#Incorrect. A portfolio is built from selected creative work.
}

::CW_U05_L07_GP_Q02::A draft has a confusing ending and several spelling errors. What should the writer address first?{
=A. Revise the ending so the reader understands the character's decision.#Correct. Meaning and purpose should be fixed before final polishing.
~B. Correct spelling only and ignore the ending.#Incorrect. Polishing does not solve the larger meaning problem.
~C. Add random formatting.#Incorrect. Random formatting does not clarify the ending.
~D. Write the portfolio note before revising.#Incorrect. The note should reflect the final revised piece.
}

::CW_U05_L07_GP_Q03::Which sentence is the best portfolio note?{
~A. I picked this because I like it.#Incorrect. This is too vague and gives no evidence.
~B. This was already typed, so it is ready.#Incorrect. Being typed does not prove readiness.
~C. I fixed a comma and stopped.#Incorrect. A portfolio note should explain meaningful revision and readiness.
=D. I selected this poem because feedback helped me replace a direct feeling statement with a stronger final image.#Correct. This connects selection, feedback, and revision evidence.
}

::CW_U05_L07_GP_Q04::Which item is revision evidence?{
~A. The writer says the draft exists.#Incorrect. Existence is not evidence of revision.
=B. A before-and-after sentence showing how feedback led to a stronger image.#Correct. This proves a meaningful change happened.
~C. A random font choice.#Incorrect. A font choice is not revision evidence unless tied to purpose and format.
~D. A statement that no feedback was considered.#Incorrect. That does not show revision evidence.
}

::CW_U05_L07_GP_Q05::Which checklist best fits final portfolio preparation?{
~A. Choose randomly, fix one comma, skip format, submit.#Incorrect. This skips major readiness checks.
~B. Publish the rough draft because first drafts are always strongest.#Incorrect. Portfolio readiness requires revision.
=C. Select with a reason, diagnose needs, revise, check format and conventions, write a portfolio note.#Correct. This matches the lesson sequence.
~D. Delete all evidence of revision.#Incorrect. Revision evidence supports portfolio readiness.
}
